<h1>Newton's Laws of Motion</h1>

<p>Isaac Newton (1643–1727) published his three laws of motion in <em>Principia Mathematica</em> (1687), one of the most influential books in the history of science. These laws describe how forces affect the motion of objects and remain valid for all everyday (non-relativistic) situations.</p>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>A force</strong> is a push or pull that can change an object's state of motion. Force is a <em>vector</em> quantity — it has both magnitude and direction. The SI unit of force is the <strong>Newton (N)</strong>. 1 N = 1 kg·m/s².
</div>

<h2>Newton's First Law — The Law of Inertia</h2>

<p><strong>Statement:</strong> An object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues in motion with constant velocity (constant speed in a straight line), unless acted upon by a net external force.</p>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Key concept — Inertia:</strong> Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist any change in its state of motion. The greater the mass of an object, the greater its inertia.
</div>

<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>A book resting on a table stays at rest — the table's normal force and gravity cancel, giving a net force of zero.</li>
  <li>A passenger lurches forward when a bus brakes suddenly — their body's inertia keeps it moving forward even as the bus decelerates.</li>
  <li>A satellite orbiting Earth keeps moving in a nearly circular path because in the near-vacuum of space, there is very little friction to slow it down.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Net Force:</strong> The first law refers to the <em>net</em> (resultant) force — the vector sum of all forces acting on an object. If all forces cancel out (ΣF = 0), the object is in <strong>equilibrium</strong> and its velocity does not change.</p>

<h2>Newton's Second Law — F = ma</h2>

<p><strong>Statement:</strong> The net force acting on an object equals the mass of the object multiplied by its acceleration.</p>

<div class="formula">
  <strong>ΣF = m × a</strong><br>
  Net Force (N) = Mass (kg) × Acceleration (m/s²)
</div>

<p><strong>What this tells us:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>For a given mass, a larger net force produces a larger acceleration.</li>
  <li>For a given net force, a larger mass produces a smaller acceleration.</li>
  <li>Acceleration is in the same direction as the net force.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1: Horizontal Force on a Box</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A 5 kg box sits on a frictionless surface. A horizontal force of 20 N is applied to it. What is the acceleration of the box?</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>ΣF = m × a</p>
  <p>20 N = 5 kg × a</p>
  <p>a = 20/5 = <strong>4 m/s²</strong> (in the direction of the force)</p>
</div>

<h3>Worked Example 2: Weight</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>What is the weight (gravitational force) of a 70 kg person on Earth? (g = 9.8 m/s²)</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>Weight W = m × g = 70 kg × 9.8 m/s² = <strong>686 N</strong></p>
  <p>Note: Weight is a force (in Newtons), not the same as mass (in kilograms).</p>
</div>

<h3>Worked Example 3: Net Force with Friction</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A 10 kg crate is pushed with a force of 50 N across a floor. The friction force opposing the motion is 20 N. What is the acceleration?</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>Net force: ΣF = Applied force − Friction = 50 N − 20 N = 30 N</p>
  <p>a = ΣF / m = 30 N / 10 kg = <strong>3 m/s²</strong></p>
</div>

<h2>Free Body Diagrams</h2>

<p>A <strong>free body diagram (FBD)</strong> is a sketch that shows all the forces acting on a single object. Drawing an FBD is the essential first step in solving any Newton's Law problem.</p>

<p><strong>Steps to draw a free body diagram:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Draw the object as a simple box or dot.</li>
  <li>Draw arrows representing each force acting on the object. Arrow length represents magnitude; direction represents direction of the force.</li>
  <li>Label each force (weight W, normal force N, applied force F, friction f, tension T, etc.).</li>
  <li>Choose a coordinate system (usually x horizontal, y vertical).</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common Forces in Free Body Diagrams</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Force</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Direction</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Weight</td><td>W or F<sub>g</sub></td><td>Downward</td><td>m × g; Earth's gravitational pull on the object</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Normal Force</td><td>N or F<sub>N</sub></td><td>Perpendicular to surface</td><td>Support force from a surface</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Applied Force</td><td>F or F<sub>app</sub></td><td>As applied</td><td>Force exerted by a person or object</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Friction</td><td>f or F<sub>f</sub></td><td>Opposite to motion (or tendency)</td><td>Resists sliding between surfaces</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Tension</td><td>T</td><td>Along string/rope</td><td>Pulling force through a rope or cable</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Friction</h2>

<p><strong>Friction</strong> is the force that opposes relative motion (or tends toward motion) between two surfaces in contact. It arises from microscopic irregularities in surfaces.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Static friction (f<sub>s</sub>):</strong> Acts when an object is not moving. Can increase up to a maximum value: f<sub>s,max</sub> = μ<sub>s</sub> × N, where μ<sub>s</sub> is the coefficient of static friction.</li>
  <li><strong>Kinetic friction (f<sub>k</sub>):</strong> Acts when an object is sliding. f<sub>k</sub> = μ<sub>k</sub> × N. Kinetic friction is usually less than maximum static friction (μ<sub>k</sub> &lt; μ<sub>s</sub>).</li>
</ul>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>N</strong> is the normal force (not the net force). The frictional force depends on how hard two surfaces are pressed together, not on the area of contact.
</div>

<h2>Newton's Third Law — Action and Reaction</h2>

<p><strong>Statement:</strong> For every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force. If object A exerts a force on object B, then object B exerts an equal and opposite force on object A.</p>

<div class="formula">
  F<sub>A on B</sub> = −F<sub>B on A</sub>
</div>

<p><strong>Critical points:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Action-reaction pairs always act on <em>different</em> objects — never on the same object.</li>
  <li>They are always equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.</li>
  <li>The fact that forces are equal does not mean they produce equal accelerations — that depends on the masses of the objects.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Walking:</strong> You push backward on the ground (action); the ground pushes you forward (reaction). Without friction, you could not walk.</li>
  <li><strong>Rocket propulsion:</strong> The rocket expels hot gas downward (action); the gas pushes the rocket upward (reaction).</li>
  <li><strong>Swimming:</strong> You push water backward with your hands (action); water pushes you forward (reaction).</li>
  <li><strong>Recoil:</strong> A gun pushes a bullet forward (action); the bullet pushes the gun backward (reaction) — this is felt as recoil.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applying Newton's Laws: Problem-Solving Strategy</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Draw a clear free body diagram for each object.</li>
  <li>Choose a convenient coordinate system. Align one axis with the direction of motion or the net force when possible.</li>
  <li>Write Newton's Second Law for each axis: ΣF<sub>x</sub> = ma<sub>x</sub> and ΣF<sub>y</sub> = ma<sub>y</sub>.</li>
  <li>Solve the resulting equations for the unknown(s).</li>
  <li>Check units and physical reasonableness of your answer.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 4: Object on an Inclined Plane</h3>
<div class="worked">
  <h4>Problem</h4>
  <p>A 3 kg block rests on a frictionless inclined plane making a 30° angle with the horizontal. What is its acceleration down the incline?</p>
  <h4>Solution</h4>
  <p>Along the incline: ΣF = W sin30° = mg sin30°</p>
  <p>a = g sin30° = 9.8 × 0.5 = <strong>4.9 m/s²</strong> down the incline.</p>
  <p>Perpendicular to incline: N = mg cos30° (no acceleration perpendicular to surface).</p>
</div>

<h2>Summary Table</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Law</th><th>Statement</th><th>Equation</th><th>Key Concept</th></tr>
  <tr><td>First (Inertia)</td><td>Objects maintain their state of motion if net force = 0</td><td>ΣF = 0 ↔ a = 0</td><td>Inertia; equilibrium</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Second (F = ma)</td><td>Net force = mass × acceleration</td><td>ΣF = ma</td><td>Cause of acceleration</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Third (Action-Reaction)</td><td>Forces come in equal and opposite pairs on different objects</td><td>F<sub>AB</sub> = −F<sub>BA</sub></td><td>Forces always in pairs</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Newton's laws tell us that objects do not need a force to <em>keep moving</em> — they need a force to <em>change</em> their motion. The net force determines the acceleration. Every force has an equal and opposite counterpart acting on a different object.
</div>
<div id="live-file-panel" style="display:none;margin:20px 0;padding:16px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:6px;background:#f9f9f9;">
  <strong>Live File</strong>
  <div id="live-file-content" style="margin-top:8px;color:#555;"></div>
</div>
<script>
(function(){
  const parts = window.location.pathname.split('/').filter(Boolean);
  const apiBase = 'http://172.31.184.39:8000';
  let course = '', week = '';
  if(parts.length >= 3 && parts[0] === 'courses'){
    course = parts[1];
    week = parts[2];
  } else if(parts.length >= 2 && parts[0] !== 'courses'){
    course = parts[0];
    week = parts[1];
  } else {
    return;
  }
  course = course.trim();
  week = week.trim();
  if(!course || !week) return;
  const panel = document.getElementById('live-file-panel');
  const content = document.getElementById('live-file-content');
    function renderFile(meta){
    if(!meta){
      panel.style.display = 'none';
      return;
    }
    const url = apiBase + '/live-file/' + course + '/' + week;
    const dlUrl = url + '?download=1';
    const type = meta.content_type || '';
    const safeName = meta.filename || 'file';
    let html = '<div><a href="' + dlUrl + '" style="color:#2572F5;text-decoration:underline;cursor:pointer;">Download ' + safeName + '</a></div>';
    if(type.startsWith('image/')){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-img');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><img id="live-file-img" src="" alt="' + safeName + '" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;"/></div>';
    } else if(type === 'application/pdf'){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-iframe');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><iframe id="live-file-iframe" src="" style="width:100%;height:480px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;"></iframe></div>';
    } else if(type.startsWith('text/')){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-iframe');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><iframe id="live-file-iframe" src="" style="width:100%;height:320px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;background:#fff;"></iframe></div>';
    }
    content.innerHTML = html;
    panel.style.display = 'block';
  }
  async function load(){
    try{
      const r = await fetch(apiBase + '/admin/live-file/' + course + '/' + week);
      if(!r.ok){renderFile(null);return;}
      const d = await r.json();
      renderFile(d || null);
    }catch{
      renderFile(null);
    }
  }
  load();
  setInterval(load, 15000);
})();
</script>
